## <div style="background-color:#1a5276; color:white; padding:15px; text-align:center; font-size:24px; font-weight:bold;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Town Operates – Lesson # 1</div>
<div style="background-color:#f0f0f0; color:#333; padding:10px; text-align:center; font-size:16px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></div>

## <span style="color:#1a5276; font-size:20px; font-weight:bold;">LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE</span>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Town Operates</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify a speaker's point of view in a simple presentation or lecture. Interactive: Discuss information from audiovisual presentations like videos or movies.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify the speaker's perspective and intentions in presentations on local issues, such as public work projects or environmental initiatives (e.g., "The council announced plans to improve the water system to address shortages. What is the intended impact of this change?"). Can interpret and discuss details from audiovisual presentations on community topics (e.g., "According to the speaker, I believe she means that by discussing recycling, we can help people change their habits, which is important."). Can ask and answer questions about main ideas in video presentations (e.g., "What did you think the main idea was?" "I think it was about the structure of town management.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

## <span style="color:#1a5276; font-size:20px; font-weight:bold;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</span>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color:#e6e6e6;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; text-align: left; color:#1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left; color:#1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left; color:#1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various local community activities, public spaces (parks, libraries), and people interacting (e.g., at a community meeting, volunteering). Students listen as the teacher briefly describes each image and asks questions like, "What kind of public service is this?" "Who do you think makes decisions about this project?" or "How can citizens participate in this?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>town council, citizens, participate, public services, decisions, responsibilities, involvement</i>) by clearly pronouncing them and using them in simple sentences related to the images. For instance, "The <b>town council</b> is responsible for many <b>public services</b>." "<b>Citizens</b> must <b>participate</b> in voting." The teacher models the pronunciation of /ɪ/ in words like <i>civic, public, community</i> and /e/ in <i>share, serve</i>, emphasizing word stress in terms like "participation" and "responsibility."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'public services' mean? Is it something for a few people or for everyone in the community?" (Expected answer: "For everyone.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you 'participate' in something, are you just watching or are you actively involved?" (Expected answer: "Actively involved.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short (1.5-2 minutes) audio clip of a local radio announcement or a segment from a simulated town council meeting. The audio discusses a new community project (e.g., improving a local park or a recycling initiative) and invites citizen feedback. The language used includes passive voice (e.g., "Decisions are made," "Projects are funded"), modals (e.g., "Citizens should vote," "You can contribute"), and target vocabulary (e.g., <i>municipal government, projects, sustainability, involvement</i>). Genre: Public Service Announcement/Informal Presentation. Register: Formal but accessible. Format: Audio recording.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listen to understand the main points and specific details in presentations or discussions about local governance and community issues.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening (First Listen):</b> Students listen once and answer a main idea question: "What is the main topic of the announcement/meeting segment?" (e.g., "A new park project" or "Community recycling efforts.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Listening (Second Listen):</b> Students listen again and complete True/False statements or match information. Examples:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: The council decided on the project without public feedback."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Match the project goal with its description: A) Improve local parks – B) Promote sustainability."</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying specific grammar structures (passive voice, modals) and target vocabulary from the audio input, focusing on attentive listening for accuracy.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher provides a transcript of a few sentences from the audio with blanks for specific vocabulary words (e.g., <i>decisions, involvement, operate, provide, elections</i>) and grammar structures (passive voice verbs, modals). Students listen to the audio segment again and fill in the blanks.
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "New policies ________ (implement) soon." (passive voice)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Citizens ________ (must/should) vote to have a say." (modal)</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Identifying Reported Speech/Viewpoints):</b> The teacher plays 3-4 short (10-15 seconds) additional audio snippets where different hypothetical citizens express opinions about community issues (e.g., "One resident said, 'Our public services need more funding!'" or "Another mentioned, 'I believe everyone should participate in surveys.'"). Students listen and identify the speaker's viewpoint, practicing reported speech by rephrasing what they heard. E.g., "The resident said that public services needed more funding." "She believed that everyone should participate in surveys."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Check and Discussion):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the previous activities. They listen to each other's reported speech sentences and discuss any discrepancies, using phrases like "What did you think the speaker's main idea was?" or "According to the speaker, I believe she means..." This promotes attentive listening to peers and using formal language in civic discussions.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task using language in a realistic scenario.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion following a new listening input. Students will listen to a short video clip (1-2 minutes) presenting two contrasting perspectives on a local issue (e.g., a debate on whether to build a new community center or repair existing infrastructure). They will then discuss the speakers' points of view and intentions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Video Presentation:</b> The teacher plays a short video segment from a news report or a simulated town hall meeting where two different viewpoints on a specific local issue (e.g., "The council announced plans to improve the water system to address shortages versus building a new road") are presented. Students listen carefully, taking brief notes on each speaker's main arguments and perceived intentions.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Group Discussion:</b> In groups of 3-4, students discuss the video. They are prompted to identify each speaker's point of view and intentions. They must use the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>projects, decisions, sustainability, responsibilities</i>), grammar (passive voice, modals, reported speech), and pragmatic functions (asking for opinions, sharing viewpoints). Formal language (sociolinguistic) is encouraged.
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompts: "What did you understand to be the main argument for the new community center?" "According to the speaker, I believe she means that by improving the water system, they can ensure sustainability, which is essential." "What do you think are important duties for citizens when these decisions are being made?" "The mayor said that new policies would be implemented soon, what was the speaker's perspective on this?"</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Class Sharing:</b> Each group briefly shares one key point from their discussion regarding a speaker's intention or perspective, ensuring they use reported speech.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to identify speaker's viewpoint, interpret intentions, and discuss details from audiovisual presentations, along with their use of target language and communicative strategies.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students remain in their groups. The teacher plays one final short audio statement (30-45 seconds) from a community leader summarizing the importance of citizen involvement in local issues. Students then collectively draft one sentence that identifies the speaker's main point of view and one sentence that explains the intended impact of their message, using reported speech and key vocabulary. One student from each group presents their sentences.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening Comprehension:</b> Accurate identification of the speaker's point of view and intention.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Language Use:</b> Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>civic, involvement, decisions, responsibilities</i>), passive voice, modals, and reported speech.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of key words, especially those with /ɪ/ and /e/ sounds, and appropriate word stress.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Clear and concise articulation of ideas.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>5. Collaboration:</b> Effective teamwork in reaching a consensus.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of identifying a speaker's point of view in today's activities?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now in discussing information from community presentations? (Scale of 1-5, 5 being very confident)"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new information did you learn about how our town operates and the role of citizens?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the skills you practiced today (listening for viewpoints, discussing community topics) be useful in your daily life?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The listening activities effectively engaged students, and most were able to grasp the main ideas and some specific details. The practice with reported speech helped reinforce understanding of speaker viewpoints. However, some students found it challenging to differentiate between direct quotes and interpretations when using reported speech; more modeling or sentence frames may be needed. The pronunciation practice of /ɪ/ and /e/ was successful, but consistent word stress on multisyllabic vocabulary needs continued reinforcement.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a written text that further elaborates on "How Our Town Operates" and "Our Town Council." This text will use the same core vocabulary and grammatical structures, providing another modality to reinforce understanding of community involvement and civic duties.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>